<DocsLayout
  title="Connectors"
  subtitle="How nikcli connects external services, stores credentials, exposes connector tools, and receives bot webhooks."
  description="Documentation for Nikcli connectors, credential storage, connector tools, webhooks, and auth flows."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="overview" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      Connectors are service-specific integrations configured under <code>connectors</code> in
      <code>nikcli.json</code>. The runtime lives in <code>packages/nikcli/src/connectors</code>.
      It checks credentials, runs health checks, creates AI SDK tools for services with operations,
      exposes prompts, and caches status/tool results.
    </p>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-8">
      <DocCard title="Tool connectors" eyebrow="Agent">
        <p>Figma, Slack, GitHub, and Lovable expose operations as dynamic agent tools.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Bot connectors" eyebrow="Webhook">
        <p>Discord, Teams, Google Chat, Linear, GitHub, and Slack are also used by chatbot webhook routes.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Credential store" eyebrow="Secrets">
        <p>Saved connector credentials are written to <code>Global.Path.data/connectors-auth.json</code> with <code>0600</code> on Unix.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Config first, auth second" eyebrow="Resolution">
        <p>Environment flags win, then inline config credentials, then saved connector auth records.</p>
      </DocCard>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="types" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Connector types</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Credential</th><th>Health check</th><th>Agent tools</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>figma</code></td><td>Personal access token</td><td><code>FigmaApi.getMe</code></td><td>File, components, styles, comments.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>slack</code></td><td>Bot token</td><td><code>SlackApi.listUsers</code></td><td>List channels, send message, search, channel info, thread reply.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>github</code></td><td>Personal access token</td><td><code>GithubApi.getUser</code></td><td>Repo, file, issue, issue list, code search, repo list.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>lovable</code></td><td>Token or API key</td><td><code>LovableApi.getProjects</code></td><td>Projects, project, chats, send message, files, run prompt.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>discord</code></td><td>Bot token</td><td>No-op health check</td><td>No connector tools; bot/webhook use.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>teams</code></td><td>Bot token</td><td>No-op health check</td><td>No connector tools; bot/webhook use.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>gchat</code></td><td>Bot token</td><td>No-op health check</td><td>No connector tools; bot/webhook use.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>linear</code></td><td>Bot token</td><td>No-op health check</td><td>No connector tools; bot/webhook use.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="add-auth" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Add and authenticate</h2>
    <p>
      <code>nikcli connectors add</code> patches either the current project config or global config.
      If you provide a credential during the wizard, it writes the credential to the connector auth
      store, not into the config file. <code>auth</code> and <code>logout</code> manage that store.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
nikcli connectors add
nikcli connectors list
nikcli connectors auth github
nikcli connectors logout github
    </DocCode>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>connectors add</code></td><td>Select scope, name, type, enabled state, optional credential.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>connectors list</code></td><td>Show configured connector status and saved credential hint.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>connectors auth [name]</code></td><td>Save a token, bot token, or API key for the selected connector.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>connectors logout [name]</code></td><td>Remove saved credentials from <code>connectors-auth.json</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="credentials" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Credential resolution</h2>
    <p>
      <code>resolveCredential</code> uses a strict order: environment flag, config inline credential,
      saved auth record, then no credential.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Env flag</th><th>Config field</th><th>Saved field</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>figma</code></td><td><code>NIKCLI_FIGMA_TOKEN</code></td><td><code>token</code></td><td><code>token</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>github</code></td><td><code>NIKCLI_GITHUB_TOKEN</code></td><td><code>token</code></td><td><code>token</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>slack</code></td><td><code>NIKCLI_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN</code></td><td><code>botToken</code></td><td><code>botToken</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>lovable</code></td><td><code>NIKCLI_LOVABLE_TOKEN</code> or <code>NIKCLI_LOVABLE_API_KEY</code></td><td><code>token</code> or <code>apiKey</code></td><td><code>token</code> or <code>apiKey</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Inline config credentials are supported but not preferred">
      <p>
        The CLI wizard stores credentials separately so project config can be shared without tokens.
        Use inline credentials only when you intentionally want config-managed secrets.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="tools" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Tool operations</h2>
    <p>
      Tool-capable connectors generate names by prefixing the configured connector name and operation
      name. For a connector named <code>github</code>, operation <code>github_get_repo</code> becomes
      <code>github_github_get_repo</code>.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Connector</th><th>Operations</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Figma</td><td><code>figma_get_file</code>, <code>figma_get_components</code>, <code>figma_get_styles</code>, <code>figma_get_comments</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Slack</td><td><code>slack_list_channels</code>, <code>slack_send_message</code>, <code>slack_search_messages</code>, <code>slack_get_channel_info</code>, <code>slack_reply_to_thread</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>GitHub</td><td><code>github_get_repo</code>, <code>github_get_file</code>, <code>github_create_issue</code>, <code>github_list_issues</code>, <code>github_search_code</code>, <code>github_list_repos</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lovable</td><td><code>lovable_get_projects</code>, <code>lovable_get_project</code>, <code>lovable_get_chats</code>, <code>lovable_send_message</code>, <code>lovable_get_project_files</code>, <code>lovable_run_prompt</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Each operation is wrapped as a dynamic tool with a JSON schema built from the operation args.
      Results are returned as strings or pretty JSON.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="bots" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Bot connectors</h2>
    <p>
      Bot/webhook platforms are mounted under <code>/chatbot</code>. The route file exposes webhook
      handlers for Discord, Slack, Teams, Google Chat, Linear, and GitHub. These are separate from
      connector tool operations even when they share the same service name.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Webhook route</th><th>Platform</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>POST /chatbot/discord/:name</code></td><td>Discord</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /chatbot/slack/:name</code></td><td>Slack</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /chatbot/teams/:name</code></td><td>Microsoft Teams</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /chatbot/gchat/:name</code></td><td>Google Chat</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /chatbot/linear/:name</code></td><td>Linear</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /chatbot/github/:name</code></td><td>GitHub</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="api" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>API routes</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>GET /connectors/status</code></td><td>Return status for configured connectors.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /connectors/:name/auth</code></td><td>Set token, bot token, or API key for a connector.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>DELETE /connectors/:name/auth</code></td><td>Remove connector auth entry.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /connectors/:name/invalidate</code></td><td>Invalidate cached status/tools for one connector.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCode language="bash">
curl http://127.0.0.1:4096/connectors/status

curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:4096/connectors/github/auth \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '&#123;"token":"ghp_example"&#125;'

curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:4096/connectors/github/invalidate
    </DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="cache" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Caches and invalidation</h2>
    <p>
      Connector status and tool creation are cached based on the active config. Auth writes,
      removals, and explicit invalidation should clear the relevant cache so the next list/tool call
      rechecks credentials and health.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Cache</th><th>Invalidated by</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Status</td><td><code>invalidateStatusCache</code> and connector invalidation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tools</td><td><code>invalidateToolsCache</code> and connector invalidation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>One connector</td><td><code>invalidateConnectorCache(name)</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="config" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Config examples</h2>
    <DocCode language="json" title="connectors config">
&#123;
  "connectors": &#123;
    "github": &#123;
      "type": "github",
      "enabled": true
    &#125;,
    "figma-design": &#123;
      "type": "figma",
      "enabled": true
    &#125;,
    "slack-builds": &#123;
      "type": "slack",
      "enabled": true
    &#125;,
    "linear-bot": &#123;
      "type": "linear",
      "enabled": true
    &#125;
  &#125;
&#125;
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      Use distinct names when the same service is used for different contexts, such as
      <code>slack-builds</code> and <code>slack-support</code>. Tool names include the connector name,
      so a descriptive name helps the model choose the correct integration.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="failure-modes" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Failure modes</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>needs_auth</code></td><td>No env, config, or saved credential resolved.</td><td>Run <code>nikcli connectors auth &lt;name&gt;</code> or set the provider env flag.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>failed</code></td><td>Health check failed or connector type is unknown.</td><td>Check token scope and service API response.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tools missing</td><td>Connector disabled, not authenticated, or type has no operations.</td><td>Check status and remember bot-only connectors have no agent tools.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Old credential still used</td><td>Status/tool cache was not invalidated after credential change.</td><td>Invalidate connector or restart the process.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lovable token ignored</td><td>Credential stored as API key but type resolution expected token.</td><td>Lovable supports token fallback to API key; verify saved field and config field.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="source-anchors" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Source anchors</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>File</th></tr></thead>
